infer a gain or cause from one score or noisy changes."}, "disposition": {"experiment_id": "k_loop_schedule", "status": "proposed", "source_hash": "bf47e981d23289bfc6faba38d6ac5a3c8362db81f2b61e0215dd441135878685", "name": "libxsmm-k-loop-software-pipeline", "hypothesis": "Preloading the next K operands into z4\u2013z7 before the current FMOPA group may hide load latency. Each loop uses one prologue load, 511 lookahead iterations, four register copies per iteration, and one final FMOPA drain. Pointer advances and FP32 accumulation order are preserved, with no load beyond K=511. Only the two selected compute loops change; the BSD notice and fallback remain intact. This source differs from the supplied measured reference. Assembly, correctness and performance are untested; validation requires the fixed flags, tolerance, general-size checks and repeated unchanged controls.", "measured_outcome": {"status": "passed", "scores": [730.6016211251696, 778.1662624286882, 756.8683079169249], "control_scores": [795.0698209044307, 1015.3586799322641, 847.4679057889266], "promoted": false, "adjudication": {"decision": "reject", "reason": "Recorded correctness checks passed, but eligible=false and promoted=false. Candidate throughput was 730.6\u2013778.2 GFLOP/s versus paired controls of 795.1\u20131015.4 GFLOP/s. It lost all three pairs by 8.1%, 23.4%, and 10.7%. Repeat spreads were 47.6 GFLOP/s for the candidate and 220.3 for controls. Control variation prevents a precise causal claim; these results provide no evidence for promotion. Discard this candidate, retain the unchanged LIBXSMM incumbent, and preserve the pending swarm order."}, "reports": ["/Users/vishnuv/Documents/Documents/weavehacks/evidence/cpu-libxsmm-measure-prepared-2026-09-24/search/trial-001/validation-0.json", "/Users/vishnuv/Documents/Documents/weavehacks/evidence/cpu-libxsmm-measure-prepared-2026-09-24/search/trial-001/validation-1.json", "/Users/vishnuv/Documents/Documents/weavehacks/evidence/cpu-libxsmm-measure-prepared-2026-09-24/search/trial-001/validation-2.json"], "comparison_identity": {"tree_hash": "76db49356748c18dccac376bed489268b047b60a", "config": {"n": 512, "tolerance": 0.002, "machine": "MacBook-Pro-8.local"}, "runtime": {"compiler": "Apple clang version 21.0.0 (clang-2100.1.1.101)", "compiler_flags": ["-O3", "-march=native", "-ffast-math", "-shared", "-fPIC", "-lm"], "architecture": "arm64", "platform": "Darwin"}}, "public_correctness": {"schema_version": "sera-cpu-correctness-v1", "seed": 20260924, "sizes": [0, 1, 3, 15, 16, 17, 31, 32, 33, 63, 64, 65, 96, 127, 129, 512], "input_kinds": ["random", "identity", "zero"], "tolerance": 0.002, "compiler_flags": ["-O3", "-march=native", "-ffast-math", "-shared", "-fPIC", "-lm"], "timing_claim": false, "source_hash": "bf47e981d23289bfc6faba38d6ac5a3c8362db81f2b61e0215dd441135878685", "passed": true, "stage": "correctness", "worst_relative_error": 1.0017720606112918e-06}}}}, {"proposal": {"experiment_id": "address_generation", "recommendation": "Experiment: change only the two full-size K loops, `Llibxsmm_478` and `Llibxsmm_70c`, in the generated 512 path. Replace the per-iteration `sub x8, x8, #1` / `cbnz x8, ...` countdown with a K pointer end test: compute `x0_end = x0 + 512*2048` and `x3_end = x3 + 512*256` before the loop, then advance `x0` and `x3` as now and branch while either pointer has not reached its end. Keep the existing pointer rewind after each loop and leave the general-size fallback unchanged. Hypothesis: removing the decrement and its dependency from each K iteration may reduce loop-control work. Validate correctness at n=512 against tolerance 0.002, then test general sizes, odd tails, and n<=0 through the unchanged fallback. Measure repeated candidate and unchanged-control scores under the fixed benchmark and AC-power procedure; report spread and do not infer a gain from one sample.", "risks": "The two streams have different strides, so both end addresses must be computed without overflow and correspond to exactly 512 iterations; an incorrect end comparison can overrun inputs or shorten the product. The end test may not be faster than the current countdown and could add address-generation work. Keep the BSD-3-Clause notice and all benchmark and ABI constraints unchanged."}, "disposition": {"experiment_id": "address_generation", "status": "proposed", "source_hash": "8b01ee298ba0f0d49915cec8e0730ff168af64562bb9d6f717f7adbaaba5ff3a", "name": "libxsmm-k-loop-pointer-end-tests", "hypothesis": "Replacing the countdown with pointer end tests may change loop-control cost; no speedup is established. Use caller-saved x4 and x5 for end addresses, respectively 1048576 and 131072 bytes beyond each loop's starting pointers. Both loops retain 512 ordered FP32 updates and their existing pointer rewinds. Only the two selected loops change; ABI, streaming transitions, packing, fallback and license remain unchanged. This implementation is absent from the supplied measured history. Assembly, correctness and performance remain untested. Validate n=512, general sizes, odd tails and n<=0 under the fixed gates. Measure repeated paired controls on the supplied current battery power with settings unchanged; report spread and keep AC results separate.", "measured_outcome": {"status": "passed", "scores": [765.6808535378168, 1018.8927819450573, 954.298077756665], "control_scores": [824.6864888803946, 868.4898760325931, 801.8983294144035], "promoted": false, "adjudication": {"decision": "revise", "reason": "Recorded correctness checks passed, but eligible=false and promoted=false prohibit adoption. Candidate scores were 765.7\u20131018.9 GFLOP/s versus paired controls of 801.9\u2013868.5. Paired changes were \u22127.2%, +17.3%, and +19.0%; repeat spreads were 253.2 and 66.6 GFLOP/s, respectively. These mixed results do not establish a repeatable gain or its cause. No repeat reached 1800 GFLOP/s. Retain the unchanged LIBXSMM incumbent and preserve the pending swarm order; treat pointer-end testing as an unproven hypothesis for future review."}, "reports": ["/Users/vishnuv/Documents/Documents/weavehacks/evidence/cpu-libxsmm-measure-prepared-2026-09-24/search/trial-002/validation-0.json", "/Users/vishnuv/Documents/Documents/weavehacks/evidence/cpu-libxsmm-measure-prepared-2026-09-24/search/trial-002/validation-1.json", "/Users/vishnuv/Documents/Documents/weavehacks/evidence/cpu-libxsmm-measure-prepared-2026-09-24/search/trial-002/validation-2.json"], "comparison_identity": {"tree_hash": "76db49356748c18dccac376bed489268b047b60a", "config": {"n": 512, "tolerance": 0.002, "machine": "MacBook-Pro-8.local"}, "runtime": {"compiler": "Apple clang version 21.0.0 (clang-2100.1.1.101)", "compiler_flags": ["-O3", "-march=native", "-ffast-math", "-shared", "-fPIC", "-lm"], "architecture": "arm64", "platform": "Darwin"}}, "public_correctness": {"schema_version": "sera-cpu-correctness-v1", "seed": 20260924, "sizes": [0, 1, 3, 15, 16, 17, 31, 32, 33, 63, 64, 65, 96, 127, 129, 512], "input_kinds": ["random", "identity", "zero"], "tolerance": 0.002, "compiler_flags": ["-O3", "-march=native", "-ffast-math", "-shared", "-fPIC", "-lm"], "timing_claim": false, "source_hash": "8b01ee298ba0f0d49915cec8e0730ff168af64562bb9d6f717f7adbaaba5ff3a", "passed": true, "stage": "correctness", "worst_relative_error": 1.0017720606112918e-06}}}}, {"proposal": {"experiment_id": "sme_fp32_tiles", "recommendation": "Experiment: change only the two generated-reference K-loop blocks, `Llibxsmm_478` and `Llibxsmm_70c`. Replace their current single-step schedule with a two-step unroll: issue two paired `LD1W` loads for each operand, then issue eight `FMOPA` operations into the same four ZA.S tiles (ZA0.S\u2013ZA3.S). Advance both pointers by two K steps per iteration and retain a separate one-step cleanup for odd K. This tests whether reducing loop-control overhead helps the two main FP32 outer-product loops while preserving the four-tile mapping and output/store blocks. Hypothesis only; no performance gain is established.", "risks": "The interleaved loads may increase live-register pressure or reduce scheduling efficiency. Pointer updates and loop bounds can skip or repeat a K step; the odd-K cleanup is required for general sizes. Check assembly and ABI/streaming-state balance, verify exact FP32 results within the fixed 0.002 tolerance for n=512 and odd/tail sizes, then measure under the unchanged benchmark with repeated scores and paired unchanged controls. Do not attribute a noisy score difference to this change. Preserve the BSD-3-Clause notice."}, "disposition": {"experiment_id": "sme_fp32_tiles", "status": "proposed", "source_hash": "ec83ff3b94f2983c17a1f7fd69aea377ed143b5993635a8770818124d4fcdd91", "name": "libxsmm-k-loop-two-step-unroll", "hypothesis": "The swarm-selected two-step unroll tests whether changing loop-control and load scheduling improves throughput. Each iteration loads two K steps, then performs eight FP32 FMOPA operations on the existing four ZA.S tiles, preserving each accumulator's K order. Separate one-step cleanup handles an odd remaining count; the generated path still uses K=512, and general sizes use the unchanged fallback. Only the two K-loop blocks change. ABI, streaming transitions, packing, stores and the complete BSD notice remain unchanged. This implementation is absent from the supplied measured history. Assembly, correctness and performance are untested; no gain is established. Validate under the fixed gates and use repeated paired unchanged controls on battery power with settings unchanged.", "measured_outcome": {"status": "passed", "scores": [1087.5141231535335, 1047.5529938845925, 1091.2009939850639], "control_scores": [947.5574885566502, 1040.1128771679143, 873.31584564557], "promoted": false, "adjudication": {"decision": "revise", "reason": "Recorded correctness checks passed, but eligible=false and promoted=false prohibit adoption. Candidate scores were 1047.6\u20131091.2 GFLOP/s versus paired controls of 873.3\u20131040.1. Paired gains were 14.8%, 0.7%, and 24.9%; repeat spreads were 43.6 and 166.8 GFLOP/s, respectively. All three pairs improved, but the smallest gain is within observed variation. These results do not establish a reliable gain or its cause. No repeat reached 1800 GFLOP/s. Retain the unchanged LIBXSMM incumbent and preserve the pending swarm order. Carry the two-step unroll forward as an unproven hypothesis for subsequent swarm review."}, "reports": ["/Users/vishnuv/Documents/Documents/weavehacks/evidence/cpu-libxsmm-measure-prepared-2026-09-24/search/trial-003/validation-0.json", "/Users/vishnuv/Documents/Documents/weavehacks/evidence/cpu-libxsmm-measure-prepared-2026-09-24/search/trial-003/validation-1.json", "/Users/vishnuv/Documents/Documents/weavehacks/evidence/cpu-libxsmm-measure-prepared-2026-09-24/search/trial-003/validation-2.json"], "comparison_identity": {"tree_hash": "76db49356748c18dccac376bed489268b047b60a", "config": {"n": 512, "tolerance": 0.002, "machine": "MacBook-Pro-8.local"}, "runtime": {"compiler": "Apple clang version 21.0.0 (clang-2100.1.1.101)", "compiler_flags": ["-O3", "-march=native", "-ffast-math", "-shared", "-fPIC", "-lm"], "architecture": "arm64", "platform": "Darwin"}}, "public_correctness": {"schema_version": "sera-cpu-correctness-v1", "seed": 20260924, "sizes": [0, 1, 3, 15, 16, 17, 31, 32, 33, 63, 64, 65, 96, 127, 129, 512], "input_kinds": ["random", "identity", "zero"], "tolerance": 0.002, "compiler_flags": ["-O3", "-march=native", "-ffast-math", "-shared", "-fPIC", "-lm"], "timing_claim": false, "source_hash": "ec83ff3b94f2983c17a1f7fd69aea377ed143b5993635a8770818124d4fcdd91", "passed": true, "stage": "correctness", "worst_relative_error": 1.0017720606112918e-06}}}}, {"proposal": {"experiment_id": "cache_blocking", "recommendation": "Experiment: test 64\u00d732 output macroblocks in the 512 assembly. Keep the current 32\u00d732 FMOPA kernel and K loop. For each 64-row band, compute two adjacent 32-row C tiles for one 32-column B panel before advancing to the next B panel; retain the existing row-major-to-column-major operand mapping and cover all tiles once. Hypothesis: a smaller output macroblock may reduce scratch or B-panel working-set pressure. The supplied cache sizes alone do not show that cache traffic limits performance. Compare this distinct traversal against the incumbent with fresh paired controls under the fixed benchmark and promotion rules.", "risks": "Changing tile order and block dimensions can break panel addressing or omit/repeat C tiles. The smaller panel may add loop overhead or worsen locality. Preserve the ABI, FP32 operation, streaming transitions, per-call allocation rules, fallback, and BSD notice. Check tolerance at n=512 and general sizes, including odd tails and n<=0. Treat the hypothesis as unverified until repeated paired measurements clear the existing noise rules; do not attribute a joint result to this experiment alone."}, "disposition": {"experiment_id": "cache_blocking", "status": "abstained", "reason": "The selected traversal already exists in the measured LIBXSMM reference. With x0=B and x1=A, the outer loop advances by 64 output rows. Each inner iteration computes two 32\u00d732 tiles: the second uses C+65536 bytes (32 rows) and scratch+128 bytes (32 FP32 values). It then advances B and C by 128 bytes (32 columns) and resets the scratch pointer. This is the requested 64\u00d732 macroblock traversal with the existing K loops. Implementing it would repeat the tested reference. No edits or performance claims are made."}}, {"proposal": {"experiment_id": "vector_access", "recommendation": "Experiment: in the LIBXSMM-generated K loops, test replacing the current load-then-add pointer updates with post-indexed contiguous loads, keeping predicates, loaded values, and FMOPA order unchanged. The hardware profile states SME2 and 16 FP32 lanes; it does not establish operand alignment, so this experiment must not assume aligned inputs. Hypothesis: post-index addressing may reduce separate address updates. This is unverified advice, not a performance claim.", "risks": "Confirm Apple clang 21 accepts the exact SME2 load form and that its post-index behavior advances by the intended bytes. Audit pointer rewind and row traversal in both loops; preserve all valid lane bounds and avoid reads past each row. Check generated assembly and run the fixed correctness suite, including general sizes, tails, and n<=0. Then use fresh paired measurements against unchanged controls on the current power source and report spread. Reject any gain that does not meet the existing eligibility and promotion rules. Do not infer a cause from instruction count or noisy scores."}, "disposition": {"experiment_id": "vector_access", "status": "abstained", "reason": "Cannot implement the selected experiment: SME2 predicated paired LD1W has no post-index writeback form. The two operand pointers require advances of 2048 and 256 bytes per K step; the loads cannot perform those updates. Substituting another load scheme would change the selected experiment. No edits or tests were performed; no performance claim is made."}}]
