{"packing-review-luna.md": "# Untested AOT-kernel directions for the next Sera swarm\n\nThe measure-prepared run rejected or declined promotion of the software-pipeline, pointer-end, and two-step K-unroll variants; noisy controls also limit causal claims. Its README says the next selected cache-blocking/vector-access ideas were not implemented because one already existed in the reference and the other required an unsupported paired-load form. `agent/state.json` records 71 model calls and 6 implementation attempts. No packing, output-store, or tile-order change was measured in that completed run.\n\nThe n=512 path in [`editable/kernel.c`](../cpu-libxsmm-reference-2026-09-24/editable/kernel.c) calls the fixed AOT LIBXSMM kernel. Do not mistake the general-size fallback for the timed path: only `n != 512` calls `fallback_gemm`, which allocates a full `n*n` A-transpose buffer. The AOT path enters SME once, reserves 128 KiB of aligned stack scratch per outer block, transposes/repacks one operand into that scratch with a ZA row/column transfer loop (`Llibxsmm_78`), then feeds it through paired `LD1W` loads and four `FMOPA`s per K step (`Llibxsmm_70c`). The other operand is loaded directly from the caller's matrix. Its four ZA tiles are zeroed for beta=0; C is not read.\n\n1. **Pack a bounded B panel alongside the existing A scratch panel.** Test packing a small K-by-32-column B panel so both operands arrive contiguous to the K loop, reusing the B panel across adjacent M tiles where the traversal permits. Include packing in the timed call and compare panel sizes that leave L1 capacity for C and other live data. Current M4 P-core L1D is 128 KiB; the AOT path already reserves 128 KiB of scratch, so simply adding another full-sized panel is not a sound design. This differs from the existing one-operand ZA transpose and from rejected K-loop changes.\n\n2. **Change how the four ZA tiles reach C.** The current path extracts ZA horizontal slices into Z-register groups with `MOV`, then issues paired `ST1W` stores for adjacent vectors and advances row addresses by 0x800 bytes. It already has beta=0 write-only output and does not use the earlier proposed ZA-to-SVE-indirect store idea as a new feature\u2014that transfer is present. A distinct test is SME ZA slice-to-memory stores, or a rearranged store grouping that removes some ZA-to-Z transfer/address instructions while preserving the same 32x32 tile and each C element's single write. Total output bytes cannot fall below one write per element; the hypothesis is lower store instruction cost. Verify the exact SME2 form and all output rows before timing.\n\n3. **Revisit the output macro-tile traversal to reuse packed data and nearby C lines.** The existing assembly has a 512-count outer loop stepped by 64 (`Llibxsmm_64`) and a 512-count nested tile loop stepped by 32 (`Llibxsmm_440`); it prepares scratch for the outer block and computes fixed 32x32 ZA tiles. Test one alternate M/N tile order or a two-adjacent-tile macro-step that keeps a packed panel live and writes neighboring 128-byte C row segments close together. Do not change the K schedule or infer cache-set conflicts. This is an unmeasured cache/reuse hypothesis; keep allocation and packing costs inside the hill call.\n\nAll candidates must preserve the AOT 512 dispatch and general-size fallback, beta=0 semantics, 0.002 numerical tolerance, and existing single-thread frozen hill. No performance gain is established by this note.\n", "swarm-review-luna.md": "# Swarm review: reduce repeated and infeasible proposals\n\nThe second round spent both selected implementation calls on stop responses, not measurements. `cache_blocking` proposed the existing 64-by-32 output traversal; the coordinator compared it against the baseline and abstained. `vector_access` proposed post-indexed SME2 paired loads; the coordinator reported that the predicated paired `LD1W` form has no such writeback encoding and abstained. See the saved responses in [round 2](../cpu-libxsmm-measure-prepared-2026-09-24/agent/state.json) and the per-call records for [`cache_blocking`](../cpu-libxsmm-measure-prepared-2026-09-24/agent/round-002/cache_blocking/call-001/response.json) and [`vector_access`](../cpu-libxsmm-measure-prepared-2026-09-24/agent/round-002/vector_access/call-001/response.json).\n\nThe board contained proposals that did not establish a distinct legal source change. The records do not isolate the cause of the ranking choices. `kernel_advisory.py` asks each advisor for a \u201cdistinct experiment\u201d and then asks all advisors to rank every non-abstaining proposal. The source and measured history were supplied, but there was no concise map of the incumbent\u2019s existing schedule and no requirement to prove that a proposal changes it or is legal for the target assembler. The source dump is large: the incumbent already has 64-row outer bands, paired 32-row output tiles for 32-column panels, and a 512-step K reduction (`editable/kernel.c`, labels `Llibxsmm_64`, `Llibxsmm_440`, `Llibxsmm_478`, and `Llibxsmm_70c`; see lines 69\u201375, 318\u2013345, 482\u2013509, and 648\u2013660). The full source was present in the prompt, but the useful facts were buried in it.\n\nIn the completed run, `cache_blocking` and `vector_access` reached the board and ranked in the selected batch. The implementation coordinator was the first stage to check the traversal against the source and the load form against target support; both then stopped without a source change or measurement. `vector_access` had also proposed a conditional post-increment-load idea in round 1, but it was not selected then. Root has since added prior implementation dispositions, including proposal IDs and stop reasons, to shared advisor, voter, and implementation context; that closes the feedback gap for selected-but-unimplemented proposals. It does not replace a compact source map: proposals can still describe unselected, already-present behavior or restate a semantically similar idea under a new ID.\n\nFor the next prompt, keep the current advisor roster, shared-board aggregation, order policy, budgets, and evaluator gates. Add a short, hash-bound source map and a round-to-round proposal ledger to the existing task context. For example:\n\n> **Incumbent facts to check before proposing:** The current generated kernel already walks 64-row outer bands with two 32-row output tiles per 32-column panel (`Llibxsmm_64`, `Llibxsmm_440`; the existing stop analysis is in the `cache_blocking` response). Its K loop already issues predicated paired `LD1W` loads, separate pointer adds, four FMOPA updates, and a loop counter (`Llibxsmm_478`, `Llibxsmm_70c`). Do not propose these existing behaviors under a new label. Each proposal must name the exact source labels/lines it changes, describe present and replacement control flow, and say why the result is not already present in the supplied source or prior proposal ledger. If that cannot be shown, abstain.\n>\n> **Feasibility check:** A ranked proposal must include the exact target-supported instruction form or source-grounded implementation path needed for its change. Rank every board ID as required; give lower priority to instruction-form ideas whose legality is conditional or unknown, and explain the uncertainty. The current local review found no post-index writeback form for SME2 predicated paired `LD1W`; do not repeat it unless supplied primary ISA or Apple clang 21 assembler evidence establishes an exact legal form. Do not substitute a different experiment after ranking.\n>\n> **Prior outcomes:** `k_loop_schedule` (software pipeline with register copies): measured below paired controls; reject. `address_generation` (pointer-end tests): mixed paired results, not eligible; do not re-propose the same pointer-end mechanism. `sme_fp32_tiles` (two-step unroll): candidate improved each pair but missed separated-ranges-plus-5% eligibility; do not call it accepted. `cache_blocking` (64x32 output traversal) was rejected as already present in the incumbent. `vector_access` (post-indexed paired predicated loads) was rejected as unsupported. New schedule experiments must state a concrete difference from these entries.\n\nThis is prompt-context guidance only. It does not add a feasibility gate to scoring or change which specialists vote. The existing deterministic ranking still chooses among the valid proposals; clearer source and feasibility facts help advisors avoid sending it duplicate or unimplementable proposals.\n"}
